  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 240 PM Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Scattered to numerous showers and thunderstorms \ncould result in a localized urban and small stream flooding threat \nthis afternoon/tonight across central North Carolina, shifting to \nthe I-95 corridor for Monday afternoon.\n\nA surface low is currently sitting over Ohio with its cold front \nextending south into Georgia while an upper level trough is near the \nIndiana/Ohio border. Both the surface and upper level features are \nhelping to bring moist flow out of the southwest with precipitable \nwater values over 2\" over the eastern two-thirds of North Carolina. \nWhile the radar currently shows cluster of showers and thunderstorms \nmoving from S-SW to N-NE along the I-95 corridor, there are \nadditional clusters of showers and thunderstorms around the \nCharlotte metro area and extending farther south into South Carolina \nand Georgia. The 12Z HREF shows that the precipitation currently to \nthe west and southwest will shift into central North Carolina later \nthis afternoon into the evening, and a corridor from Raleigh down to \nFayetteville appears to be the most likely to see some isolated \nhigher amounts of rainfall. Confidence is not high enough to issue a \nflood watch anywhere as there is still a bit of uncertainty in the \nmodels as to where an axis could develop, but for this afternoon and \nevening, it appears that the axis may manage to develop across two \nurban areas, which would be more sensitive to heavier rainfall. \nRainfall coverage and rates should begin to drop off by midnight, \nwith little activity expected overnight into Monday morning. The \naxis of heavier rainfall is expected to become more northeast-\nsouthwest oriented Monday afternoon, somewhere likely along the I-95 \ncorridor. Both Sunday and Monday have central North Carolina in a \nmarginal (level 1 of 4) risk for excessive rainfall, with the \nhigher/slight risk contained to the immediate North Carolina \ncoastline each day. Areas from the Triangle and just to the west are \nalso under a marginal (level 1 of 5) risk for severe weather this \nafternoon, with the threat coming from damaging wind. However, the \nSPC mesoanalysis page shows that the higher downdraft CAPE is \ncontained to the VA/NC mountains, and considering forecast soundings \nin central North Carolina do not show much in the way of stronger \nwind gusts to mix to the surface, do not think that the severe \nthreat will be much of an issue locally.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Mainly diurnally driven showers and storms expected \nTuesday through Friday.\n\nAloft, expect persistent deep swly flow over the region, with a \ntrough to the west and Bermuda high pressure to the east. The s/w \nenergy within the trough will split, with the nrn stream trough \nlifting newd, while a low cuts off over the TN Valley/srn \nAppalachians by mid-week. While the cutoff low and Bermuda high \nremain in place, another trough with embedded shortwaves will \namplify over the Plains then slowly progress ewd across the upr MS-\nValley and Great Lakes through Sat, becoming less amplified and \ncontinuing ewd across the Northeast over the weekend. At the \nsurface, expect continued swly flow and advection of warm, moist air \ninto the area through the week. An area of low pressure should lift \nnewd across the area Tue/Tue night. A weak trough may remain over \nthe area, while the Bermuda high ridges westward into the region \nthrough the week and into the weekend. GFS forecast soundings \nindicate highest PWATs on Tue, around or above 2\" across much of the \narea, with 500 J/Kg or less of instability and ~20 kts or less of \nshear. There will be a chance for generally diurnally driven showers \nand storms through the rest of the week. The GFS is drier than the \nEC, especially late week into the weekend. All of central NC is in a \nMarginal Risk from the WPC for excessive rainfall on Tue.",
